## Congratulations!

You've completed Chapter 1: Karel the Robot! Along the way, you've learned the same fundamental programming concepts that every programmer uses:

- **Sequential execution** — code runs one line at a time, top to bottom
- **Functions** — reusable blocks of code that you name and call
- **Decomposition** — breaking complex problems into simpler pieces
- **Loops** — repeating actions (while loops for conditions, for loops for counting)
- **Conditionals** — making decisions based on the current state
- **State** — the world remembers what your program has done, and a program that can read that state can make plans

These concepts aren't just for Karel — they're the foundation of **all programming**. In the next chapter you'll start writing "real" Python: variables, strings, numbers and the rest. Karel's beepers-as-memory tricks will retire, but the algorithms you built out of them won't.

For now, feel free to revisit any lesson, try the exercises again, or experiment in the Karel Playground where you can design your own worlds!

</LessonShell>
